\begin_layout Standard
Using the explicit SVD enumerated types (ENABLE,
 XOSC_Clksrc,
 clk_ref) makes your bare-metal setup highly readable and using the aggregate others => <> correctly satisfies Ada’s strict safety requirements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now that this hardware configuration block compiles cleanly the Pico 2 W clock tree will properly scale up to the external 12 MHz crystal on boot which ensures that the PROC0 tick engine begins pulsing every microsecond,
 satisfying the hardware real-time timer requirement that the Ravenscar task scheduler relies on to manage task switches and delay until timers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A Crucial Step to Check
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before flashing code to the Pico 2 W check how the onboard wireless LED is handled.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the wireless Pico 2 W the green onboard LED is not physically wired to a standard RP2350 microcontroller pin (unlike the standard Pico 2 which uses GPIO 25).
 Instead,
 it is routed through the Infineon CYW43439 wireless chip via an internal SPI interface.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a concurrent task loop is trying to toggle a direct microcontroller pin like Pico.LED or a raw GPIO register via SVD the task will execute perfectly but the physical LED on the board won’t light up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
How to Verify That Tasks Are Running
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To confirm that a multi-tasking scheduler is actively looping without having to initialize the entire wireless SPI network stack use a physical test setup:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Wire an external LED and a 220Ω resistor to a physical pin such as GPIO 15.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Configure that pin as an output using GPIO SVD or HAL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Update the task body to toggle that specific GPIO pin instead of the internal LED.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the external LED flashes then the Ravenscar runtime,
 hardware initialization routine and background task loop are working flawlessly.
\end_layout

